 a different side of the room,

12
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,620
 to dig into issues we face within our own gender,

13
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:28,940
 while growing an understanding for the opposite sex.

14
00:00:28,940 --> 00:00:31,680
 - One night, we're putting the guys under the microscope.

15
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:33,220
 - The next night is for the girls.

16
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:35,460
 But don't worry, everybody's invited every night.

17
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:37,020
 - Because here's the thing we figured out.

18
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:39,580
 - We're not actually short on opinions about men and women.

19
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:40,500
 - He's saying this.

20
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:41,500
 - She's saying that.

21
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:44,300
 - But what does God have to say about all of it?

22
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:46,620
 - Welcome to the debate that culture can't settle.

23
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:48,380
 - Welcome to what's actually true.

24
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:49,220
 About him.

25
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:50,040
 - And about her.

26
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:52,980
 - Welcome to your favorite Tuesday night series.

27
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:54,380
 - He said, she said.

28
00:00:54,380 --> 00:00:58,580
 (audience cheering)

29
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,380
 - All right, well, Porch, how are we doing tonight?

30
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:01,900
 We doing okay?

31
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:03,380
 Great to see you.

32
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:05,100
 Full house, everybody is in the room.

33
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:07,940
 It's so great to have everybody here,

34
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:11,460
 as well as everybody that is tuning in from the chapel.

35
00:01:11,460 --> 00:01:12,700
 Like, shout out to you guys.

36
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:15,360
 Can we give some love to the overflow tonight?

37
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:16,700
 (audience cheering)

38
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:18,020
 And then can we give some love

39
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:19,580
 to everybody tuning in everywhere?

40
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:21,500
 Like, wherever it is that they might be watching from,

41
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:22,380
 all across the country.

42
00:01:22,380 --> 00:01:25,300
 We've got Porch Live locations that want to be a part

43
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:26,940
 of what God is doing here.

44
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:31,140
 Not just in this room and not just amongst these people,

45
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:32,940
 but amongst our generation.

46
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:36,040
 Like, it is a genuine honor to be a part of it.

47
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,620
 Like, to play a small role in a really big movement

48
00:01:38,620 --> 00:01:40,980
 of what God is doing in the lives of young adults.

49
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:43,580
 Special shout out to Porch Live Scottsdale.

50
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:45,140
 Anybody from Scottsdale in the house?

51
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:46,500
 Got anybody from Arizona?

52
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,860
 Maybe one, maybe two.

53
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:48,860
 Fort Worth.

54
00:01:48,860 --> 00:01:49,900
 (audience cheering)

55
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,020
 As always, some of you guys with us here.

56
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:53,900
 And then Porch Live Dayton.

57
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:57,300
 Give it up for Porch Live Dayton, everybody.

58
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,760
 Come on.

59
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:01,300
 Hey, it's amazing to be back.

60
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:02,900
 We started a brand new series last week.

61
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:04,380
 It looks like some of you might've been in the room.

62
00:02:04,380 --> 00:02:05,860
 Others of you got the memo.

63
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:09,300
 Last week, the boys were on the chopping block.

64
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:12,700
 This week, it's the ladies' turn.

65
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:15,980
 (audience cheering)

66
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:18,080
 Let me start off by saying this.

67
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:20,820
 When I got married, it did not take me long

68
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:25,820
 before a profound realization struck me.

69
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:27,980
 And it was not that marriage takes work.

70
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:30,200
 It's not that communication is required.

71
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:33,220
 It's not that working between families,

72
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:36,300
 your family and her family, can be sanctifying.

73
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:39,500
 Really, what struck me was just how different

74
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,100
 men and women were.

75
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:43,060
 And it did not take me long to realize this.

76
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:46,660
 And I suspected this was the case through my single years

77
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:48,660
 and then into my dating experience.

78
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,820
 But it was within my first year of marriage

79
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:52,820
 that it began to really settle in on me

80
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:55,940
 just how different we were in fact.

81
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:56,760
 It didn't take long.

82
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,660
 Like before Brooke and I even said I do,

83
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:01,640
 she got the keys to our apartment.

84
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,400
 She moved in because she wanted to nest.

85
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,340
 And I didn't really necessarily know what that meant.

86
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:08,900
 In my mind, moving in and getting a house settled

87
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:10,780
 basically meant we needed a mattress

88
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:12,780
 and a television and some wifi.

89
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:14,460
 But then I showed up and it looked like

90
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:17,260
 we had an HGTV intervention.

91
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:20,980
 Because everything was everywhere.

92
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:24,420
 Like there were blankets thrown over every item

93
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:26,300
 or furniture piece that we had.

94
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:27,260
 There were curtains hung.

95
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:29,660
 I had never had curtains in my life.

96
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:32,100
 There were books in all of the shelves

97
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:33,980
 and they were ordered by color.

98
00:03:33,980 --> 00:03:35,820
 Spines that I had never before seen

99
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:37,860
 but aesthetically looked very pleasing.

100
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:39,660
 There were candles burning

101
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:42,040
 and there were pictures on the walls

102
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:43,580
 and somehow there were plants still alive.

103
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:45,020
 I remember thinking to myself,

104
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:46,760
 are we actually supposed to live here?

105
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:48,620
 It looks like it looks way too nice

106
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:51,340
 for someone the likes of myself to move in.

107
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:52,740
 And then we moved on after that.

108
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:56,940
 And I saw that we were supposed to combine our furniture.

109
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:59,700
 At least that's what I thought.

110
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:01,740
 Because in reality, my furniture quietly thanked me

111
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:02,740
 for my years of service

112
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:05,380
 and it headed to the donation center.

113
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:06,820
 And then there were the decorative pillows.

114
00:04:06,820 --> 00:04:07,940
 I don't know if anybody's ever talked to you

115
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:09,420
 about decorative pillows, guys.

116
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:11,180
 I did not know much about decorative pillows

117
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:12,860
 but apparently those are the pillows

118
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:14,580
 that they get moved off of the bed

119
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:16,900
 so the real pillows can come and they can work

120
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:17,940
 through the night shift.

121
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:20,580
 And then you take the real pillows and you put those away

122
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:22,860
 so you can put the decorative pillows back on the bed.

123
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:24,780
 That way you never have the opportunity

124
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:26,660
 or make the mistake of enjoying them.

125
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:28,060
 And then there came grocery shopping.

126
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:29,900
 I bought protein, like that was my experience.

127
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:31,980
 I bought chicken and rice and eggs and peanut butter.

128
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:34,740
 Like that was a good week's worth of groceries.

129
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:36,140
 But then whenever I got married,

130
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:39,220
 I realized our grocery list not only increased

131
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:42,180
 and our grocery budget also increased.

132
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:44,540
 I realized the items diversified.

133
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:47,100
 Like we had flowers in our home,

134
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:49,940
 which feels like the most meaningless purchase possible

135
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:52,580
 because you're just throwing cash away in that moment.

136
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:53,500
 That stuff's going to die.

137
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:54,980
 And then we had laundry beads.

138
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:56,540
 Like I knew that my clothes needed to be clean

139
00:04:56,540 --> 00:04:58,460
 but I didn't know that they actually needed to smell good.

140
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:02,100
 And then we had hand soap that matched the seasons.

141
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:05,380
 I didn't even know that hand soap had seasons.

142
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:08,580
 And then don't get me started on kitchen utensils.

143
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:11,100
 I owned one spatula my entire time in college.

144
00:05:12,220 --> 00:05:15,980
 And I flipped thousands of eggs with that spatula.

145
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:18,260
 It was basically family to me.

146
00:05:18,260 --> 00:05:23,020
 But then we got married and we had lots of spatulas.

147
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:24,260
 We had lots of spoons.

148
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:25,820
 We had teaspoons and soup spoons

149
00:05:25,820 --> 00:05:27,340
 and we had small spoons and big spoons.

150
00:05:27,340 --> 00:05:29,100
 We had several different types of forks

151
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:30,220
 because some forks are for salad

152
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:32,740
 and other types of forks are for the entrees.

153
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:37,100
 And then we had loads and layers

154
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:39,200
 of different types of utensils.

155
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:41,140
 You see, my first year of marriage taught me

156
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:44,420
 in convincing fashion that men and women are different.

157
00:05:44,420 --> 00:05:47,060
 Can I get an amen from anybody in the house?

158
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:48,620
 (congregation laughing)

159
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:50,740
 And while it was funny to me at first,

160
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:54,240
 learning all of these little nuances and differences,

161
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:56,580
 eventually it became pretty beautiful to me.

162
00:05:56,580 --> 00:05:59,680
 And the reason for that is because I learned

163
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:04,220
 that where I was weak, Brooke was strong.

164
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:07,380
 And when Brooke was struggling, I could support.

165
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:12,140
 I realized that we in fact were better together.

166
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:15,300
 Our differences, they were not problematic to the two of us.

167
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:18,100
 They were actually purposeful.

168
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:19,460
 Meaning despite what culture says,

169
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:22,940
 we do not need to erase our distinctions.

170
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:24,620
 We talked about this last week with the fellows,

171
00:06:24,620 --> 00:06:26,460
 but men don't need to quell their ambition.

172
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:27,580
 They don't need to scrap their strength

173
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:30,340
 and they don't need to apologize for being male

174
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:33,340
 for fear of being seen as some sort of gigachat.

175
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:36,020
 I said this last week, men, you need to hear me

176
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:39,380
 because this is the calling card every single week.

177
00:06:39,380 --> 00:06:41,080
 We talk about your manhood.

178
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:43,740
 Your masculinity does not need to be suppressed.

179
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:46,980
 Your masculinity needs to be sanctified.

180
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:48,300
 It needs to be sanctified.

181
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:49,540
 (congregation applauding)

182
00:06:49,540 --> 00:06:52,140
 But as we turn the corner and we talk to the women tonight,

183
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:54,660
 I want to tell you something similar.

184
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:57,220
 I want you to know that women

185
00:06:57,220 --> 00:06:59,660
 don't need to devalue motherhood.

186
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:02,200
 They don't necessarily need to prioritize career achievement

187
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:03,600
 or reject physical modesty

188
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:05,980
 because that's giving total trad wife.

189
00:07:05,980 --> 00:07:10,000
 You need to know the calling card on womanhood

190
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,300
 is that your femininity does not need to be erased.

191
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:16,020
 It needs to be embraced.

192
00:07:16,020 --> 00:07:18,180
 It's fascinating.

193
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:22,580
 We live in a cultural moment where many people,

194
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:27,580
 particularly women, assume equality requires sameness,

195
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:30,900
 that men and women should be interchangeable.

196
00:07:30,900 --> 00:07:35,240
 That's why toxic masculinity of the past,

197
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:37,680
 which was marked by things like promiscuity

198
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:41,140
 and hyper-independence and vulgarity and sexual entitlement

199
00:07:41,140 --> 00:07:45,940
 have actually become the repackaged trademarks

200
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:47,340
 of female empowerment.

201
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,420
 The truth of the matter is

202
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:51,780
 I don't really need to stand here and prove it to you

203
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:53,660
 because our culture and mainstream media

204
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:56,820
 is doing an exceptional job of this instead.

205
00:07:56,820 --> 00:07:59,740
 You need to only watch a single episode of "Live Island."

206
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:01,820
 You need to only go listen to a single episode

207
00:08:01,820 --> 00:08:02,660
 of "Call Her Daddy."

208
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:05,300
 You need to only go surf around on Instagram

209
00:08:05,300 --> 00:08:07,780
 and find a social media feminist,

210
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:10,220
 and what you will see is you will see

211
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:14,340
 everything that was formerly canceled

212
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:18,300
 now consequently embraced.

213
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:22,360
 You see, modesty has been displaced by assertiveness.

214
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:24,840
 Purity has been replaced by sexual liberation,

215
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:28,600
 and quiet confidence has been eclipsed by bravado,

216
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:31,780
 and this is the sad irony of the moment.

217
00:08:31,780 --> 00:08:35,700
 Hear me on this, the world has not elevated femininity

218
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:38,380
 as it promised and said it has devalued it

219
00:08:38,380 --> 00:08:41,100
 because what the world has done

220
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:43,340
 in the efforts to accept women

221
00:08:43,340 --> 00:08:48,260
 is it has rejected what made you women to begin with.

222
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:50,660
 It has erased your distinctions.

223
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:54,180
 It has removed the very design that God gave you

224
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:59,180
 that has dignified you by Him and for us,

225
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:01,380
 and so here's my hope.

226
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:05,520
 Like, I hope to just establish there's a different way

227
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:10,440
 to process what is the purpose of womanhood,

228
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:12,540
 because what if the purpose of women

229
00:09:12,540 --> 00:09:14,440
 isn't discovered by becoming less feminine,

230
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:16,640
 but it is by embracing the woman

231
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:18,240
 that God created you to be?

232
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:20,900
 That's exactly what I wanna talk about with you tonight,

233
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:21,740
 so if you've got a Bible,

234
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:23,940
 you can turn with me to Genesis chapter two.

235
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:26,640
 While you're doing that, I said this last week,

236
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:27,940
 but it's important to return to

237
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:30,440
 just because in a room like this,

238
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:32,660
 I want you to know why we're approaching

239
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:34,960
 the topics of manhood and womanhood the way we are,

240
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:36,460
 because it's very intentional.

241
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:39,560
 You see, typically, whenever you arrive at this discussion

242
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:41,060
 in Christian environments,

243
00:09:41,060 --> 00:09:44,060
 the conversation leans towards roles

244
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:46,500
 or functions of men and women,

245
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:50,760
 and it's not difficult to understand why that would be.

246
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:52,140
 Like, if you read through the Bible,

247
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:54,400
 the Scripture has a lot to say about men

248
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:56,340
 as husbands and fathers,

249
00:09:56,340 --> 00:09:58,440
 and the Scripture has a lot to say about women

250
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:00,740
 as wives and mothers,

251
00:10:00,740 --> 00:10:02,180
 and yet here's the reason

252
00:10:02,180 --> 00:10:04,720
 why I don't just want us to focus on role,

253
00:10:04,720 --> 00:10:06,980
 because if we think about the roles of a man

254
00:10:06,980 --> 00:10:08,460
 or the roles of a woman,

255
00:10:08,460 --> 00:10:11,260
 it can give the impression that manhood and womanhood

256
00:10:11,260 --> 00:10:13,700
 are life goals to achieve

257
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:17,800
 as opposed to gifts you've already received.

258
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:21,160
 As I say that, I know that a lot of you,

259
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:23,160
 you're aspiring to be a husband.

260
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:24,660
 You're aspiring to be a wifey.

261
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:26,240
 Like, it's a part of your 10-year plan.

262
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:29,140
 It's something that you intend to accomplish at some point,

263
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:30,780
 and you absolutely should.

264
00:10:30,780 --> 00:10:33,640
 I am a husband, and I am a father.

265
00:10:33,640 --> 00:10:34,780
 I recommend it.

266
00:10:34,780 --> 00:10:37,040
 I think it is an incredible experience,

